==Status Report: 2026-10-19 10:56 PDT (Monday)==

No implementation yet — architecture proposal for review before any code.

Root cause
----------
Opening a bookmark does not reuse the existing reading screen. MainActivity is
declared with the default launchMode="standard" (the manifest element sets no
launchMode), and BookmarksActivity.openInMain() starts it with only
FLAG_ACTIVITY_SINGLE_TOP. Because MainActivity is NOT the top of the task at that
moment (BookmarksActivity is), SINGLE_TOP does nothing and Android creates a second
MainActivity instance.

Each MainActivity hosts its own MainViewModel (Compose viewModel() scoped to the
activity) and therefore its own SpeechController (the object that renders WAVs and
owns the live MediaPlayer). The first controller keeps playing. The process-wide
plumbing is a singleton — WcastMediaService.active and the SpeechControllerBridge
callbacks — and the second ViewModel's init overwrites SpeechControllerBridge.on* to
point at itself, so the media notification/session now controls only the NEW
controller. The old controller is unreachable, so both streams are audible while only
the newest one is stoppable from the notification.

openBookmarkedArticle() does call stopSpeaking(), but that stops the new ViewModel's
still-idle controller, not the first controller that is actually playing.

This also explains the "spontaneous" second report (two voices reading the same
article at different offsets): any second entry into MainActivity while it is not the
task top — the media-notification content intent in
WcastMediaService.appContentIntent() also uses only FLAG_ACTIVITY_SINGLE_TOP, and any
future deep link would behave the same — can create a duplicate controller.

Proposed fix (primary)
----------------------
Enforce a single MainActivity instance so there can only ever be one live
SpeechController.

1. Add android:launchMode="singleTask" to the <activity android:name=".MainActivity">
   element in app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml. Wcast is a single-reader app whose
   launcher activity is the reading screen, so singleTask is the right mode: every
   intent (bookmark open, notification tap, launcher, deep link) converges on the
   existing instance and is delivered through onNewIntent(), which already reads
   EXTRA_OPEN_BOOKMARK.

2. (Defensive, complementary) Make the intent flags explicit so the behavior does not
   silently depend on launch mode:
   - BookmarksActivity.openInMain(): use
     FLAG_ACTIVITY_CLEAR_TOP or FLAG_ACTIVITY_SINGLE_TOP (instead of just SINGLE_TOP).
   - WcastMediaService.appContentIntent(): add FLAG_ACTIVITY_CLEAR_TOP so a
     notification tap reuses the screen rather than stacking a second copy.

Proposed fix (small correctness reorder)
----------------------------------------
In MainViewModel.openBookmarkedArticle(), call stopSpeaking() before setting
_uiState = UiState.Loading (and before any coroutine work), so selecting a bookmark
terminates the current stream immediately rather than only after the stored copy is
loaded. With a single instance this is not strictly required, but it makes the
"stop the old stream before starting the new one" invariant obvious at the call site.

Optional hardening (defense-in-depth)
-------------------------------------
Add a process-wide "only one speaker" guard so a playing stream can never coexist with
another, independent of activity lifecycle. Minimal shape: a registry in
SpeechController that records the currently live controller, and start()/startQueue()
first calls stop() on any other live controller. Cheap, but overlaps the launch-mode
fix; recommend landing the primary fix + reorder first and adding this only if another
duplicate-stream path is still reachable.

Longer term (out of scope for this ticket)
------------------------------------------
The clean end-state is to move the single SpeechController out of the per-activity
ViewModel and into the already-singleton WcastMediaService, with ViewModels binding to
it through SpeechControllerBridge. That makes "one live TTS stream per process"
structural rather than lifecycle-dependent. It overlaps ticket 21/23 work, so noted
here but not proposed now.

